add('3',5,'Number Theory',r'An infinite sequence of positive integers increases at each step by either $54$ or $77$. Prove that some term has equal tens and units digits, allowing the ending $00$.','The two increments become one or two steps in a different residue cycle. Finding adjacent target residues then proves that no path can avoid them indefinitely.',['modular arithmetic','residue cycle','unavoidable pattern'],r'''Modulo $100$, the increment $54$ equals $2\cdot77$. Define $b_j=a_1+77j$. Each successive term of the original sequence has the same last two digits as a $b_j$, with the index increasing by either $1$ or $2$.

Since $\gcd(77,100)=1$, the residues of any $100$ consecutive $b_j$ run through all residues modulo $100$. Choose an arbitrarily large $k$ with $b_k\equiv11\pmod{100}$; then $b_{k+1}\equiv88\pmod{100}$.

An unbounded increasing index sequence with jumps only $1$ or $2$ cannot skip both consecutive indices $k,k+1$. Thus an original term ends in $11$ or $88$, as required.''')
add('4',4.5,'Combinatorics',r'Seven identical-looking coins contain exactly two counterfeits. Genuine coins have one common weight; counterfeit coins have a smaller common weight. Design three balance-scale weighings that identify both counterfeits.','The balanced and unbalanced first outcomes leave different candidate structures; a complete adaptive decision tree must resolve both in just two further weighings.',['weighing','case analysis','adaptive strategy'],appearances=[{'section':'Grade 11','number':'1'}])
