add('2',5.5,'Geometry',r'A convex cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$ satisfies $AB+CD=AD$. Prove that the internal bisectors of $\angle ABC$ and $\angle BCD$ meet on side $AD$.','The additive side condition needs a carefully chosen point on AD. Cyclic angles and an auxiliary circle then locate the two bisectors, with a tangency case requiring attention.',['cyclic quadrilateral','angle bisectors','auxiliary circle'])
ex('3',3.5,'Using x+y+z=1 factors each denominator as(x+1)(y+1); the sum becomes4/[(x+1)(y+1)(z+1)] and one AM–GM application finishes.')
add('4',4.5,'Number Theory',r'Primes $p,q$ satisfy that $p^2+pq+q^2$ is a perfect square. Prove that $p^2-pq+q^2$ is prime.','A difference-of-squares factorisation restricts the prime factors sharply. The apparent primality conclusion is obtained by determining the input primes.',['prime factorisation','difference of squares','Diophantine equation'],r'''Write $p^2+pq+q^2=k^2$. The case $p=q$ would make $3$ a rational square, so assume $p<q$. Then $k<p+q$ and
$$(p+q-k)(p+q+k)=pq.$$
The larger factor exceeds $q$, so the factors must be $1,pq$. Adding gives $2(p+q)=pq+1$, equivalently $(p-2)(q-2)=3$.

The case $p=2$ is impossible. Therefore $p-2=1$ and $q-2=3$, giving $(p,q)=(3,5)$. The required expression equals $9-15+25=\boxed{19}$, which is prime.''',notes='The linked official solution has a typographical error in the final factorisation; the independent exposition corrects it.')
add('5',5.5,'Algebra',r'Start with polynomials $P=x^2+2$ and $Q=x+1$. Repeatedly adjoin the sum, difference or product of any two available polynomials, allowing the same polynomial twice. Can one obtain (a) $x^3+2$, (b) $x^4+2$?','An evaluation modulo a prime supplies an obstruction, but the attainable target requires an explicit polynomial construction using only the permitted operations.',['polynomial invariant','modular evaluation','construction'],r'''Every available polynomial has value divisible by $3$ at $x=-1$: this holds for $P(-1)=3,Q(-1)=0$ and is preserved by all three operations. Since $(-1)^3+2=1$, part(a) is impossible.

For part(b), first form $R=Q^2-P=2x-1$. The permitted operations then produce
$$P^2-R^2-R-Q-Q=x^4+2,$$
as direct expansion confirms. Thus part(b) is possible.''',notes='The original solution contains a numerical typo in its modular evaluation; the exposition uses x=−1 to make the obstruction transparent.')


