ex('3',3.5,'Distinct reception counts would be0 through n−1; the maximum forces all other vertices to have sent, contradicting the minimum and minimum degree2.')
add('4',4.5,'Combinatorics',r'Twelve blank digit positions form four three-digit integers whose product will be taken. At each turn G chooses a nonzero decimal digit and M chooses an unfilled position for it, with both players seeing all previous choices. Prove M can force the final product to be divisible by $9$.','An online placement strategy must cope with adversarial digit choices. Sorting residues into partially filled factors guarantees two multiples of three before capacity becomes an issue.',['online strategy','modular arithmetic','pigeonhole principle'],r'''M aims to complete two three-digit factors whose digits all have the same residue modulo $3$. Each such factor is divisible by $3$.

Until two factors are complete, keep every partially filled factor monochromatic in residue, with at most one partial factor for each residue. Place a new digit into its residue’s partial factor if there is one; otherwise start an empty factor. A completed factor is left untouched.

This strategy cannot run out of empty or suitable partial factors before the second completion. With no completed factor there are at most three partial factors. With one completed factor, if all other three are partial, all three residues already have a place. Moreover, while at most one factor is complete, at most $3+3\cdot2=9$ digits have been placed. Thus the second factor is completed by the tenth digit. Fill any remaining positions arbitrarily: the product already contains two factors divisible by $3$.''')
add('5',4.5,'Number Theory',r'Can $1,2,\ldots,2024$ be arranged around a circle, each once, so that for every three consecutive entries $a,b,c$, the integer $b+1$ divides $a+c$?','Parity propagates around the entire circle and forces an alternating pattern; only then does the largest entry create a sharp size contradiction.',['cyclic arrangement','parity','extremal principle'])
