ex('1',2.5,'All fifty odd numbers work by parity; fifty-one selected numbers force one of the adjacent pairs(1,2),…,(99,100), whose difference equals their gcd.')
ex('2',3.5,'The two isosceles triangles and angle-bisector definitions reduce the desired equality to a direct angle sum, without an additional construction.')
add('3',4,'Algebra',r'Solve over the reals $$x+\frac1x=2y^2,\qquad y+\frac1y=2z^2,\qquad z+\frac1z=2x^2.$$','The system first forces positivity and a lower bound. Applying the equation at a largest variable then turns that bound into rigidity.',['cyclic system','AM–GM','maximum principle'],r'''The equations imply $x,y,z>0$, since $u+1/u$ has the same sign as nonzero $u$. AM–GM now gives $x,y,z\ge1$.

Suppose the largest variable is $x>1$, rotating the variables cyclically if necessary. Then $z\le x<x^2$ and $1/z\le1<x^2$, contradicting $z+1/z=2x^2$. Thus the largest variable is $1$, and all three are $1$. This triple satisfies the system.''')
ex('4',3,'Two average equations after moving the misclassified score give n+m=25 for the unaffected groups, hence26 students; the transfer direction follows from monotonicity.')
add('5',5,'Combinatorics',r'Some cells of a $9\times9$ board are initially coloured. A row, column or one of the two main diagonals may be entirely coloured whenever it already contains at least three coloured cells. What is the least initial number from which the whole board can become coloured?','The lower bound comes from analysing the first two activated lines, while a six-cell construction uses both diagonals to start a cascade.',['bootstrap process','extremal construction','line intersections'])
lv24('Grade 11')
ex('1',2,'The square-root domain and the upper bound immediately squeeze b to0 and a to1/4.')
add('2',4.5,'Geometry',r'In rectangle $ABCD$, $E\in BC$ and $O=AE\cap BD$. The line through $O$ parallel to $BC$ meets $AB,CD$ at $F,G$. Given $[BOF]=4$ and $[AOD]=63$, find $[OECG]$. Here brackets denote area.','Several similar triangles share one scale factor. Recovering that factor from the two given areas requires a quadratic relation before the remaining region can be calculated.',['similarity','area ratios','quadratic equation'])
add('3',4.5,'Combinatorics',r'A simple undirected graph has $n\ge3$ vertices. Any subset of vertices sends one message along each incident edge, so a vertex receives one message from each of its neighbours in that subset. Prove that two vertices receive the same number of messages.','The familiar equal-degree pigeonhole argument is insufficient because only some vertices send. Assuming all counts distinct and examining the three extreme counts produces the contradiction.',['graph theory','pigeonhole principle','extreme values'],r'''If all counts were distinct, they would be exactly $0,1,\ldots,n-1$. Let $v_i$ denote the vertex receiving $i$ messages.

Vertex $v_{n-1}$ is adjacent to every other vertex, and all those other vertices have sent. Since $v_0$ receives nothing, $v_{n-1}$ has not sent and $v_0$ has no other neighbour.

Now $v_{n-2}$ is different from both $v_0$ and $v_{n-1}$ because $n\ge3$. To receive $n-2$ messages when $v_{n-1}$ has not sent, it must be adjacent to every remaining vertex, including $v_0$. This contradicts the preceding conclusion. Therefore two counts coincide.''',notes='Equivalent graph formulation of the computer-message problem. The Grade9 version adds a minimum-degree assumption and is easier; it is assessed separately.')
add('4',5.5,'Number Theory',r'For which positive integers $n$ can one choose $2n+1$ positive integers, allowing repetitions, such that the sum of any $n+1$ selected entries is divisible by the sum of the remaining $n$ entries?','Ordering the entries makes two complementary block sums comparable. Using divisibility in both directions gives a stronger inequality than either condition alone.',['ordering','divisibility','extremal bounds'])
add('5',4,'Combinatorics',r'Players A and B alternately choose unused numbers from $1,\ldots,2n$. Each round A chooses $a$, then B chooses $b$, and records $y=x^2-ax+b$. After $n$ rounds, add the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points for all pairs of recorded parabolas. Prove B can force this sum to equal $n^3-n$.','A complementary pairing makes every recorded curve pass through one common point; counting pairs then converts the geometric condition into the target formula.',['pairing strategy','polynomial graphs','counting'])
lv24('Grade 12')
ex('1',3.5,'Subtracting equations gives(x−y)(2xy−1)=0. Equality of two variables forces all three equal, while pairwise distinctness contradicts the three product equations.')
add('2',4.5,'Geometry',r'In a scalene triangle $ABC$, points $D\in AB$ and $E\in AC$ satisfy $BD=CE$. The circles $(ABE)$ and $(ACD)$ meet again at $T$. Prove that $AT$ bisects $\angle BAC$.','The two cyclic quadrilaterals produce a congruence between triangles outside the original pair of circles. Their equal altitudes then reveal the angle-bisector locus.',['cyclic quadrilaterals','congruence','angle-bisector locus'])
add('3',6,'Combinatorics',r'Two players alternately remove one or two sweets from a pile of $200$, with A moving first. Taking the last sweet wins; a player unable to move loses. Each player may consume at most $L$ sweets in total. Who can force a win for (a) $L=140$, (b) $L=110$?','The usual modulo-three strategy must respect individual consumption budgets. In the tighter case the second player needs a delayed switch of strategy and a quantitative proof that the switch occurs before the budget is exhausted.',['games','resource constraint','strategy switching'])
ex('4',3.5,'For concatenated2^n and14^n, odd n makes C−1 divisible by3 and even n makes it end in5; each divisor is proper.')
add('5',5,'Algebra',r'Find the greatest real $A$ for which $$3x^2+y^2+1\ge A(x^2+xy+x)$$ holds for all real $x,y$.','The multiplier may be negative, so a naive ratio minimisation is unsafe. Completing a square reduces the universal inequality to a discriminant condition and a sharp equality example.',['quadratic forms','completing the square','best constant'],r'''Put $A_0=\sqrt7-1$, so $A_0^2+2A_0=6$. Then direct expansion gives
$$3x^2+y^2+1-A_0(x^2+xy+x)=\left(y-\frac{A_0x}{2}\right)^2+\left(\frac{A_0x}{2}-1\right)^2\ge0.$$
Equality occurs at $x=2/A_0$, $y=1$. At this point $x^2+xy+x>0$, so any larger coefficient $A$ would make the inequality false. Therefore the greatest value is $\boxed{\sqrt7-1}$.''')

